        <div class="guide-content" itemprop="articleBody">
            <section class="guide-summary" id="short-answer">
                <p class="guide-kicker">The short answer</p>
                <p>Libra is free to start, smoothes daily readings into a clear trend, and is maintained by an active developer. It is also ad-supported unless you pay for Pro, and it syncs data through its own cloud. WeekScale is an alternative for iOS that keeps the calm trend idea but drops the ads, the account, and the recurring price.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="libra-strengths">
                <h2>What Libra does well</h2>
                <p>Libra deserves an honest appraisal, because it is good at what it does.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>A genuinely free tier with a smooth exponential-smoothing trend line.</li>
                    <li>Tracks more than weight: body fat, muscle mass, and text notes.</li>
                    <li>BMI and body-composition analysis built into the charts.</li>
                    <li>Cloud sync, Apple Health, and Withings scale integration.</li>
                    <li>A responsive single developer with years of history.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>If you use those features every day, Libra is a reasonable choice, and you may not need to switch at all.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="prose-section" id="weekscale-differences">
                <h2>Where WeekScale differs</h2>
                <p>WeekScale makes a narrower set of choices, on purpose:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>No ads, on any tier.</strong> Libra shows ads unless you subscribe to Pro. WeekScale has no ads at all &mdash; there is no free tier with ads and no Pro tier to escape them.</li>
                    <li><strong>No cloud.</strong> Libra stores and syncs data through its own cloud service. WeekScale has no account and no WeekScale-operated cloud; your history lives on your device, and the Android app isn't even granted internet permission.</li>
                    <li><strong>Calendar weeks, not a moving trend.</strong> Instead of a continuously smoothed line, WeekScale compares fixed, finished weeks. <a class="inline-link" href="/why-calendar-weeks">Read the argument.</a></li>
                    <li><strong>Weight only, by design.</strong> WeekScale deliberately tracks weight &mdash; not body fat, muscle, or BMI &mdash; and does not set goals or predict outcomes.</li>
                    <li><strong>One-time price.</strong> $6 USD or &euro;5 EUR, once, instead of a monthly or yearly subscription.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="guide-principle">
                    <strong>A smaller app, with clearer edges.</strong>
                    <span>WeekScale drops Libra's extras in exchange for no ads, no cloud, and no subscription.</span>
                </div>
            </section>

